false,"entity":null,"inverse":null,"items":null,"max":null,"max_length":null,"min":null,"min_length":null,"properties":{},"required":false,"type":"string","values":[]},"withholds":{"acyclic":null,"additional_properties":false,"entity":null,"inverse":null,"items":null,"max":null,"max_length":null,"min":null,"min_length":null,"properties":{},"required":false,"type":"string","values":[]}}},"version":1},"entity":"story","events":[],"from_state":"draft","id":"ent-7b88e108cc35778f1f9f01e199406df0","result":{"entity":"story","fields":{"body":"## Context\n\nToday a writer finds an entity by its name, so one real-world entity can end up as two nodes of different types with the same name. AGENTS.md invariant 3: names are not identities. The resolver (A10, design § 45) takes a typed reference and answers against one canonical snapshot, deterministically, with no fuzzy or similarity matching.\n\nThe rule, as far as it is decided:\n\n- A candidate is a node of the canonical root whose `type_id` equals the reference `type_id` exactly and whose `aliases` contain one of the reference aliases, compared byte for byte. `canonical_name` is never compared.\n- One candidate: `Resolved`. None: `ProposeNew`, carrying the type and aliases; nothing is written, and the caller mints the id (`ekr mint node`). More than one: `Ambiguous`, listing every candidate in id order. The resolver never picks one.\n- Refused, not guessed: a reference with no alias (`reference-without-identity`); a reference carrying identifying keys (behind `decision-blocker:typed-reference-identifying-keys`); a reference whose type is abstract or has declared descendants (`reference-type-has-subtypes`, behind `decision-blocker:typed-reference-subtype-matching`).\n\nThe resolver is a pure function over `ekr-graph` canonical state and the `ekr-ontology` schema. It reads; it holds no writer (invariant 1).\n\n## Design sections\n\n§ 10, § 45, § 63 step 3, § 74.4; A10 (`docs/predecessors.md` § 2).\n\n## Domain relations\n\n- `Node → NodeType`, many-to-one, a reference. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/graph.yaml`, `ekr.graph.Node` relation `type`, cardinality one, via `type_id`). This is why a match compares exactly one `type_id`.\n- `GraphRoot → Node`, one-to-many, the root owns its nodes. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/graph.yaml`, `ekr.graph.GraphRoot` relation `nodes`, `owns`, via `root_id`). Candidates come from the canonical root only.\n- `Node → alias`, one-to-many, the node owns its aliases, and nothing stops two nodes holding the same alias. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/graph.yaml`, `ekr.graph.Node` field `aliases: List<String>`; this is a field, not a `relations:` entry, and no `ess/1` declaration makes an alias unique). That is why `Ambiguous` exists.\n- `NodeType → parent NodeType`, many-to-many. Inferable (`systems/ekr/domains/ontology.yaml`, `ekr.ontology.NodeType` fields `parents: List<TypeId>` and `abstract_type`). Used here only to detect and refuse a type with descendants.\n\n## Tests the story ships\n\n- Two nodes with the same `canonical_name` and different types: a reference to either type resolves to that type node only.\n- A property test: permuting the snapshot node order or the reference alias order gives byte-identical outcomes.\n- An alias differing by case or whitespace does not match.\n- The crate declares no string-similarity dependency, held by a test that reads its manifest.\n\n## Scope\n\nDerived 2026-09-27 by `story-scoper`. Every line is **cited** (read from the story or the tree) or **inferred** (a reading that could be wrong).\n\n- `crates/ekr-integrate` — cited\n- `Cargo.toml` — cited\n- `Cargo.lock` — inferred\n- `README.md` — inferred\n- `crates/ekr/tests/adversary_docs_contract.rs` — inferred\n- **Would collide with:** any unit adding or removing a workspace member (`Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock`, `README.md` § Status)\n- **Confidence:** high for the paths the story names; medium for the inferred lines\n\n## Acceptance\n\nOver one canonical snapshot, a typed reference resolves to the single node of exactly its type that holds one of its aliases, to `ProposeNew` when no node does, and to `Ambiguous` listing every candidate when more than one does, and two nodes sharing a name but differing in type never resolve to each other.\n","document":{"events":[{"args":{"changes":{"scope":[{"confidence":"inferred","path":"Cargo.lock"},{"confidence":"cited","path":"Cargo.toml"},{"confidence":"inferred","path":"README.md"},{"confidence":"cited","path":"crates/ekr-integrate"}]},"command":"update-entity","target":"ent-7b88e108cc35778f1f9f01e199406df0"},"changed":{"$aep":{"archived":false,"metadata":{"created_at":1790459649279,"id":"ent-7b88e108cc35778f1f9f01e199406df0","locator":"ep://planning/store/story/typed-reference-resolver","provenance":{"created_by":"human:timo","updated_by":"human:timo"},"revision":6,"type":"aep.story/v1","updated_at":1790460209219}},"scope":[{"confidence":"inferred","path":"Cargo.lock"},{"confidence":"cited","path":"Cargo.toml"},{"confidence":"inferred","path":"README.md"},{"confidence":"cited","path":"crates/ekr-integrate"}]},"entity":"aep.entity","from_state":"draft","id":"ent-7b88e108cc35778f1f9f01e199406df0","payload":{"actor":"human:timo","at":1790460209219,"causation":"cmd-child-ee4b6215d2acfc3c69d51f81c41e55a0cbc47a23355b7b0b4270da8b348c4c13","correlation":"protocol-artifact-scope","event_id":"aep.entity:ent-7b88e108cc35778f1f9f01e199406df0@6#0~0c159a3b566676d8","executor":null,"recorded_at":"2026-09-26T22:03:29Z"},"revision":6,"to_state":"draft","type":"aep.entity.update/v1","version":1}],"fields":{"$aep":{"archived":false,"metadata":{"created_at":1790459649279,"id":"ent-7b88e108cc35778f1f9f01e199406df0","locator":"ep://planning/store/story/typed-reference-resolver","provenance":{"created_by":"human:timo","updated_by":"human:timo"},"revision":6,"type":"aep.story/v1","updated_at":1790460209219}},"body":"## Context\n\nToday a writer finds an entity by its name, so one real-world entity can en